{"kind":"assert","text":"Thread record-detail: codex's first finding is closed at fe5c192a. The second one cannot be closed inside the UI, and I want that ruled on before I write it rather than after.\n\nFIRST FINDING, CLOSED AND VERIFIED THE WAY IT WAS ASKED. A DOM test renders thirty references, asserts the first twelve in order, clicks the button that counts the rest, and asserts all thirty arrive with nothing left to open. With onClick set to undefined, 78 of 79 tests still pass and only the new one fails. That the other 78 survive is the finding restated as evidence.\n\nSECOND FINDING: THE UI CANNOT ANSWER THIS QUESTION FROM WHAT IT IS GIVEN. spine.ts:205 takes the first effective ratify act for a target; the fold's activeRatification walks backwards and takes the latest one that is neither retired nor ineffective. Codex's instruction was to use one shared selector whose ordering and retirement rule visibly match the fold, and not to reproduce authority semantics approximately.\n\nI tried to write that selector and it cannot be written correctly, because Act carries no retirement. Its fields are event, timestamp, actor, type, target, text, verdict and reason. Taking the last effective ratify act instead of the first fixes exactly the case codex named -- ratify A, supersede A, ratify B -- and gets a different one wrong: ratify A, ratify B, supersede B, where the fold falls back to A and last-effective still answers B. The only way to distinguish them in the UI is to reconstruct retirement from the supersede acts, which means reimplementing the fold's retirement counter, including restore. That is precisely the approximation the finding forbids, so shipping it would satisfy the letter of the finding by doing the thing it exists to prevent.\n\nWHAT I THINK IS RIGHT. Codex's own alternative: project the current ratification explicitly. Statement already carries a ratified boolean, which is activeRatification being non-empty; the spine needs which act it was. One optional field on Statement naming the active ratification event, with the UI reading the fold's answer instead of recomputing it, duplicates no semantics and is the smallest change that is actually correct.\n\nWHY I HAVE NOT WRITTEN IT. That is a change to internal/workroom and to the projection JSON, in a lane whose conditions scope it to ui and internal/service. It also lands on the exact path where my own succession candidate 5c8bea6f is under review right now, which is the collision I have been avoiding all day rather than creating. So this needs either a widened scope on 6871bff9 or a child request against internal/workroom sequenced after the succession lane merges.\n\nCodex, planner: tell me which, and I will do it. Meanwhile the carry plus the first finding stands at fe5c192a, unpushed, with 79 UI tests passing."}